0.5002822919050889, \
0.0328526741435874]]\)\!\(\*StyleBox[\"to\",FontWeight->\"Bold\",FontColor->\
RGBColor[0.9904173342488747, 0.5002822919050889, \
0.0328526741435874]]\)\!\(\*StyleBox[\" \
\",FontWeight->\"Bold\",FontColor->RGBColor[0.9904173342488747, \
0.5002822919050889, 0.0328526741435874]]\)\!\(\*StyleBox[\"its\",FontWeight->\
\"Bold\",FontColor->RGBColor[0.9904173342488747, 0.5002822919050889, \
0.0328526741435874]]\)\!\(\*StyleBox[\" \
\",FontWeight->\"Bold\",FontColor->RGBColor[0.9904173342488747, \
0.5002822919050889, \
0.0328526741435874]]\)\!\(\*StyleBox[\"radius\",FontWeight->\"Bold\",\
FontColor->RGBColor[0.9904173342488747, 0.5002822919050889, \
0.0328526741435874]]\) \n\nExpress the spatial part of the metric as \n    \!\
\(\*SuperscriptBox[\(ds\), \(2\)]\) = \[Minus]a(t\!\(\*SuperscriptBox[\()\), \
\(2\)]\) h,\n\nwhere h contains everything that doesn\[CloseCurlyQuote]t \
depend on t (\[CapitalBeta], \[GothicCapitalC], \!\(\*SubscriptBox[\(\
\[GothicCapitalD]\), \(4\)]\), \[Ellipsis]). \n\nAfter \[ScriptCapitalA](t) \
is substituted, we see above that every spatial field \nequation \
\!\(\*SubscriptBox[\(R\), \(ii\)]\) contains the same term, \
-\!\(\*FractionBox[\(4  k\), SuperscriptBox[\(a\), \(2\)]]\). Thus\n\t\
\!\(\*FractionBox[\(\*SubscriptBox[\(R\), \(ii\)] \((h)\)\), SubscriptBox[\(h\
\), \(ii\)]]\) = 4k\n\nfor every spatial direction i = 1-5 (and the \
off-diagonal \!\(\*SubscriptBox[\(R\), \(ij\)]\) = 0).\n\n\!\(\*SubscriptBox[\
\(R\), \(ij\)]\)(h) is the Ricci tensor of the 5-dimensional spatial metric h \
alone.\n\n\!\(\*StyleBox[\"Definition\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\). For a \
diagonal metric, the ratio \!\(\*FractionBox[SubscriptBox[\(R\), \(ii\)], \
SubscriptBox[\(h\), \(ii\)]]\) is called the  \n\
\!\(\*StyleBox[\"Ricci\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\!\(\*StyleBox[\" \
\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\!\(\*StyleBox[\"curvature\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\
\!\(\*StyleBox[\" \
\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\!\(\*StyleBox[\"in\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\!\(\*\
StyleBox[\" \
\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\!\(\*StyleBox[\"direction\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\
\!\(\*StyleBox[\" \",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\!\(\*StyleBox[\"i\",FontWeight->\
\"Bold\"]\). \n\nGeometrically, Ricci curvature in direction i is the sum of \
the\ncurvatures of all the 2-dimensional planes that contain direction i. \n\n\
In 5 dimensions there are 4 such planes, which is where the 4 comes from.\n  \
\[Bullet] Adding it up over all directions gives the familiar curvature \
scalar, R = 5 \[CenterDot] 4k.\n\nExample. On a sphere of radius p, every \
2-plane has curvature \!\(\*FractionBox[\(1\), SuperscriptBox[\(p\), \
\(2\)]]\),   \n\t\t so a 5-sphere has Ricci curvature \
\!\(\*FractionBox[\(4\), SuperscriptBox[\(p\), \(2\)]]\) in every direction.\n\
\!\(\*StyleBox[\"Consequence\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\!\(\*StyleBox[\".\",\
FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\) \n  \[Bullet] The field equations force the Ricci \
curvature to be the\n\tsame number, 4k, everywhere and in every direction. \n \
 \[Bullet] One constant, k, controls the curvature of the whole space: \n\t\
\[Checkmark] Ordinary 3-space\n\t\[Checkmark] Curled 2-space \
(\!\(\*SuperscriptBox[\(S\), \(2\)]\))\n\n\!\(\*StyleBox[\"Why\",FontWeight->\
\"Bold\"]\)\!\(\*StyleBox[\" \
\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\!\(\*StyleBox[\"this\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\!\(\
\*StyleBox[\" \
\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\!\(\*StyleBox[\"matters\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\!\
\(\*StyleBox[\" \",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\!\(\*StyleBox[\"for\",FontWeight->\
\"Bold\"]\)\!\(\*StyleBox[\" \
\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\!\(\*StyleBox[\"6\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\!\(\*\
StyleBox[\"D_\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\!\(\*StyleBox[\"2.\",FontWeight->\"\
Bold\"]\) \n  \[Bullet] The curled \!\(\*SuperscriptBox[\(S\), \(2\)]\) of \
radius \!\(\*SubscriptBox[\(p\), \(4\)]\) is curved, with curvature \
\!\(\*FractionBox[\(1\), SuperscriptBox[SubscriptBox[\(p\), \(4\)], \
\(2\)]]\).\n  \[Bullet] Its directions must still have Ricci curvature 4k, so \
k \n\tcan\[CloseCurlyQuote]t be independent of p\:2084.\n  \[Bullet] The \
whole 5-space is a 5-sphere of radius \!\(\*SubscriptBox[\(p\), \(4\)]\), \
with \n\tRicci curvature \!\(\*FractionBox[\(4\), \
SuperscriptBox[SubscriptBox[\(p\), \(4\)], \(2\)]]\), so 4k = \
\!\(\*FractionBox[\(4\), SuperscriptBox[SubscriptBox[\(p\), \(4\)], \(2\)]]\) \
and k = \!\(\*FractionBox[\(1\), SuperscriptBox[SubscriptBox[\(p\), \(4\)], \
\(2\)]]\). \n  \[Bullet] Ordinary 3-space then shares that curvature, and the \
\n\tuniverse is closed with size about \!\(\*SubscriptBox[\(p\), \(4\)]\). \n \
 \[Bullet] (A general theorem, Myers\[CloseCurlyQuote] theorem, says the same \
thing: \n\t\[Checkmark] Positive Ricci curvature 4k in every direction forces \
\n\t  a finite space, at most \!\(\*FractionBox[\(\[Pi]\), SqrtBox[\(k\)]]\) \
across.)\n\n\
\!\(\*StyleBox[\"Contrast\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\!\(\*StyleBox[\" \
\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\!\(\*StyleBox[\"with\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\!\(\
\*StyleBox[\" \
\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\!\(\*StyleBox[\"circles\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\)\!\
\(\*StyleBox[\".\",FontWeight->\"Bold\"]\) \n  \[Bullet] A circle has no \
curvature, so a curled circle (5D, 6D_1, \n\t7D_1) doesn\[CloseCurlyQuote]t \
constrain k this way. \n  \[Bullet] That\[CloseCurlyQuote]s why the circle \
cases allow k = 0 or k free, \n\twhile the sphere cases (6D_2, 7D_2 Solution \
1, 7D_3) \n\tforce a closed universe tied to the sphere\[CloseCurlyQuote]s \
radius.\>\"", "]"}], ";"}], "\[IndentingNewLine]", "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
