\\\",FontWeight->\\\"Bold\\\"]\\)\\!\\(\\*StyleBox[\\\"direction\\\",\
FontWeight->\\\"Bold\\\"]\\)\\!\\(\\*StyleBox[\\\" \
\\\",FontWeight->\\\"Bold\\\"]\\)\\!\\(\\*StyleBox[\\\"i\\\",FontWeight->\\\"\
Bold\\\"]\\). \\n\\nGeometrically, Ricci curvature in direction i is the sum \
of the\\ncurvatures of all the 2-dimensional planes that contain direction i. \
\\n\\nIn 5 dimensions there are 4 such planes, which is where the 4 comes \
from.\\n  \[Bullet] Adding it up over all directions gives the familiar \
curvature scalar, R = 5 \[CenterDot] 4k.\\n\\nExample. On a sphere of radius \
p, every 2-plane has curvature \\!\\(\\*FractionBox[\\(1\\), \
SuperscriptBox[\\(p\\), \\(2\\)]]\\),   \\n\\t\\t so a 5-sphere has Ricci \
curvature \\!\\(\\*FractionBox[\\(4\\), SuperscriptBox[\\(p\\), \\(2\\)]]\\) \
in every direction.\\n\\!\\(\\*StyleBox[\\\"Consequence\\\",FontWeight->\\\"\
Bold\\\"]\\)\\!\\(\\*StyleBox[\\\".\\\",FontWeight->\\\"Bold\\\"]\\) \\n  \
\[Bullet] The field equations force the Ricci curvature to be the\\n\\tsame \
number, 4k, everywhere and in every direction. \\n  \[Bullet] One constant, \
k, controls the curvature of the whole space: \\n\\t\[Checkmark] Ordinary \
3-space\\n\\t\[Checkmark] Curled 2-space (\\!\\(\\*SuperscriptBox[\\(S\\), \
\\(2\\)]\\))\\n\\n\\!\\(\\*StyleBox[\\\"Why\\\",FontWeight->\\\"Bold\\\"]\\)\\\
!\\(\\*StyleBox[\\\" \
\\\",FontWeight->\\\"Bold\\\"]\\)\\!\\(\\*StyleBox[\\\"this\\\",FontWeight->\\\
\"Bold\\\"]\\)\\!\\(\\*StyleBox[\\\" \
\\\",FontWeight->\\\"Bold\\\"]\\)\\!\\(\\*StyleBox[\\\"matters\\\",FontWeight-\
>\\\"Bold\\\"]\\)\\!\\(\\*StyleBox[\\\" \
\\\",FontWeight->\\\"Bold\\\"]\\)\\!\\(\\*StyleBox[\\\"for\\\",FontWeight->\\\
\"Bold\\\"]\\)\\!\\(\\*StyleBox[\\\" \
\\\",FontWeight->\\\"Bold\\\"]\\)\\!\\(\\*StyleBox[\\\"6\\\",FontWeight->\\\"\
Bold\\\"]\\)\\!\\(\\*StyleBox[\\\"D_\\\",FontWeight->\\\"Bold\\\"]\\)\\!\\(\\*\
StyleBox[\\\"2.\\\",FontWeight->\\\"Bold\\\"]\\) \\n  \[Bullet] The curled \
\\!\\(\\*SuperscriptBox[\\(S\\), \\(2\\)]\\) of radius \\!\\(\\*SubscriptBox[\
\\(p\\), \\(4\\)]\\) is curved, with curvature \\!\\(\\*FractionBox[\\(1\\), \
SuperscriptBox[SubscriptBox[\\(p\\), \\(4\\)], \\(2\\)]]\\).\\n  \[Bullet] \
Its directions must still have Ricci curvature 4k, so k \\n\\tcan\
\[CloseCurlyQuote]t be independent of p\:2084.\\n  \[Bullet] The whole \
5-space is a 5-sphere of radius \\!\\(\\*SubscriptBox[\\(p\\), \\(4\\)]\\), \
with \\n\\tRicci curvature \\!\\(\\*FractionBox[\\(4\\), \
SuperscriptBox[SubscriptBox[\\(p\\), \\(4\\)], \\(2\\)]]\\), so 4k = \
\\!\\(\\*FractionBox[\\(4\\), SuperscriptBox[SubscriptBox[\\(p\\), \\(4\\)], \
\\(2\\)]]\\) and k = \\!\\(\\*FractionBox[\\(1\\), \
SuperscriptBox[SubscriptBox[\\(p\\), \\(4\\)], \\(2\\)]]\\). \\n  \[Bullet] \
Ordinary 3-space then shares that curvature, and the \\n\\tuniverse is closed \
with size about \\!\\(\\*SubscriptBox[\\(p\\), \\(4\\)]\\). \\n  \[Bullet] (A \
general theorem, Myers\[CloseCurlyQuote] theorem, says the same thing: \\n\\t\
\[Checkmark] Positive Ricci curvature 4k in every direction forces \\n\\t  a \
finite space, at most \\!\\(\\*FractionBox[\\(\[Pi]\\), SqrtBox[\\(k\\)]]\\) \
across.)\\n\\n\\!\\(\\*StyleBox[\\\"Contrast\\\",FontWeight->\\\"Bold\\\"]\\)\
\\!\\(\\*StyleBox[\\\" \
\\\",FontWeight->\\\"Bold\\\"]\\)\\!\\(\\*StyleBox[\\\"with\\\",FontWeight->\\\
\"Bold\\\"]\\)\\!\\(\\*StyleBox[\\\" \
\\\",FontWeight->\\\"Bold\\\"]\\)\\!\\(\\*StyleBox[\\\"circles\\\",FontWeight-\
>\\\"Bold\\\"]\\)\\!\\(\\*StyleBox[\\\".\\\",FontWeight->\\\"Bold\\\"]\\) \\n \
 \[Bullet] A circle has no curvature, so a curled circle (5D, 6D_1, \
\\n\\t7D_1) doesn\[CloseCurlyQuote]t constrain k this way. \\n  \[Bullet] \
That\[CloseCurlyQuote]s why the circle cases allow k = 0 or k free, \
\\n\\twhile the sphere cases (6D_2, 7D_2 Solution 1, 7D_3) \\n\\tforce a \
closed universe tied to the sphere\[CloseCurlyQuote]s radius.\"\>"], "Print",
